[stderr] ## Objective
[stderr] 
[stderr] Define the immutable, versioned shared campaign-state, command, result, event,
[stderr] revision, interaction-ID, and deterministic-RNG contracts needed to validate
[stderr] legal transitions. This foundation defines envelopes and invariants only; it
[stderr] does not introduce a feature state machine or authored campaign values.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Dependencies
[stderr] 
[stderr] R0002 provides the shared-core/adapter seam and R0003 provides deterministic
[stderr] fixtures. They are implementation dependencies, not authoring blockers.
[stderr] Retain GATE-0004: concrete party and content shapes remain unapproved.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Ownership
[stderr] 
[stderr] - `src/core/{state,commands,events,invariants,rng}/`.
[stderr] - The versioned campaign-snapshot envelope and deterministic RNG state.
[stderr] - The public core command, state, event, result, revision, and interaction-ID
[stderr]   interfaces, plus `tests/core/contract.*`.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Required behaviour
[stderr] 
[stderr] Make state transitions pure and explicit: a command carries an expected
[stderr] campaign revision and stable interaction/action ID; processing returns either
[stderr] a typed rejection with no mutation or ordered state/events with a new revision.
[stderr] Validate command shape, session state, identifiers, availability, eligibility,
[stderr] predicates, and expected revision before any transition. Invariant helpers
[stderr] must reject invalid state and preserve the prior immutable state.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Record deterministic RNG seed/state at safe boundaries. Require idempotency for
[stderr] retries: a duplicate interaction ID or acknowledged durable transition returns
[stderr] its prior result without a second reward, checkpoint, or revision-changing
[stderr] transition. Process one command at a time per active campaign and reject stale
[stderr] writers so visibility changes, repeated input, and concurrent tabs cannot
[stderr] interleave a transition.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Web and PWA differences
[stderr] 
[stderr] Web and PWA adapters submit the same shared command envelopes and consume the
[stderr] same state/event results. Platform adapters may debounce controls and manage a
[stderr] local slot lease/read-only warning, but neither gains authority to mutate state
[stderr] directly. Installation, cache lifecycle, and storage-driver work are outside
[stderr] this contract.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Non-goals
[stderr] 
[stderr] Do not author content values; implement combat, inventory, quest, exploration,
[stderr] or UI state machines; select party shapes; implement a save driver or cache;
[stderr] add account, identity, token, role, network, or remote state; or create assets,
[stderr] audio, maps, dialogue, or reference-derived content.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Asset assessment
[stderr] 
[stderr] `asset_impact` is `not_applicable`: these serializable state and interface
[stderr] contracts deliberately exclude authored content. Do not create or reserve
[stderr] incidental visual, source, audio, font, icon, illustration, texture, sprite,
[stderr] map, or other product assets; `asset_ids` remains blank.
[stderr] 
