## Objective

Create the bounded developer-documentation closure for the SHARD-01 foundation.
It cross-references the approved planning contracts for architecture ownership,
command flow, save-versus-cache recovery, and P0 connected-scope exclusion.
It is documentation work only: it neither implements these boundaries nor
claims their approval, product completion, or release readiness.

## Dependencies

R0002 defines the shared-core and adapter seam; R0004 owns deterministic
campaign-state transitions; R0006 owns the local save and recovery protocol;
and R0008 owns the P0 identity and connected-service exclusion. Their listed
contracts are prerequisites for this index, not evidence that implementation
or the B0004 checkpoint has been accepted. Retain GATE-0003 / DEC-0003 for
storage, quota, support, and recovery-policy choices, and GATE-0006 / DEC-0006
for any connected scope. No documented gate is resolved here.

## Ownership

- `docs/architecture.md` foundation component, dependency-direction, and
  public-boundary inventory.
- `docs/development.md` command-flow, validation, and ownership cross-reference.
- `docs/save-and-recovery.md` save-versus-preference-versus-cache boundary and
  recovery index.
- `docs/security-boundary.md` P0 local-profile and connected-scope exclusion.
- Foundation decision and ownership index, and the documented public-boundary
  inventory.

This contract owns the required documentation set and its cross-reference
index only. R0002, R0004, R0006, and R0008 retain ownership of their seams,
state, persistence protocol, negative boundary tests, and any implementation
outputs. Content validation, migration execution, PWA release/cache lifecycle,
diagnostics, assets, and audio remain with their respective current or future
contracts.

## Required behaviour

Document the foundation architecture as a shared deterministic rules core,
content validator, browser presentation/input adapters, local save/recovery
repository, PWA release manager, and error/diagnostic boundary. State the
direction of interaction: adapters issue commands, only the rules core produces
durable campaign state, and the shell renders returned state/events without
mutating campaign state. Browser APIs, timers, network calls, and renderer
dependencies remain outside the core.

Document that the save repository owns serialized snapshots and recovery
metadata, while the shell owns ephemeral UI state. Record the write-ahead
replacement boundary: validate a complete candidate and read-back before
promoting a current pointer, retain a known-good snapshot, and quarantine
invalid or incompatible candidates. Preferences and PWA cache records are not
saves and cannot promote, overwrite, delete, synchronise, or imply success for
a campaign snapshot. Migration transformations remain a later pure-runner
concern; this index must not choose storage, quota, retention, support window,
or migration policy.

Document P0 as an offline local-profile boundary with no account, token, role,
remote profile, network client, cloud save, analytics transport, entitlement,
or connected campaign authority. Treat persisted bytes, content, browser
events, URL parameters, and service-worker messages as untrusted at their
respective boundaries. Any future connected proposal remains separately gated
and cannot redefine the local campaign as non-authoritative.

## Web and PWA differences

Responsive web and installed PWA share the same core, command, local-save,
preference, quarantine, and connected-scope boundaries. The PWA release/cache
manager may manage install and cached-release lifecycle but must remain separate
from campaign persistence: a cache refresh, update, or offline failure cannot
alter a confirmed save. This documentation does not select a browser-storage
mechanism, service-worker rollout policy, browser matrix, or PWA implementation.

## Non-goals

Do not implement or approve a framework, renderer, storage API, quota,
retention or support policy, migration set, diagnostics vendor or transport,
identity, authorization, remote service, cloud sync, telemetry, purchase,
entitlement, rollout, release operation, or player-facing release guide. Do
not claim vendor, security, privacy, legal, product, or release approval. Do
not create gameplay, UI, content, assets, audio, source implementation, or
test fixtures beyond the documentation contract described above.

## Asset assessment

`asset_impact` is `not_applicable`: foundation documentation and its ownership
index need no product asset. Do not create or reserve incidental visual, source,
audio, icon, illustration, font, texture, sprite, map, or other product asset;
`asset_ids` and audio IDs remain blank.

## Verification

- Load this runbook and compare its task ID, sequence, title, catalogue ID,
  requirement IDs, source specifications, dependencies, authoring batch,
  factory stages, and asset/audio/browser decisions with the R0012 manifest
  allocation and IMP-0012 catalogue record.
- Cross-check the documentation index against R0002, R0004, R0006, and R0008:
  each prerequisite's ownership, dependency boundary, and retained gate must
  be named without transferring ownership or claiming implementation status.
- Confirm the index distinguishes durable campaign state, serialized snapshot
  and recovery metadata, preferences, PWA cache, shell UI state, and future
  connected scope; confirm it contains no storage/vendor/policy selection.
- Confirm the documentation records GATE-0003 / DEC-0003 and GATE-0006 /
  DEC-0006 as unresolved. If R0006 or B0004 is blocked, preserve that state in
  the cross-check; do not mark the foundation, a gate, or a release accepted.
- Record the B0006 checkpoint only after every comparison succeeds. On a
  mismatch or existing conflicting R0012 contract, stop, retain the allocated
  ID and path, and repair or report only this documentation contract; never
  overwrite, reuse, renumber, or replace an R ID.

## Evidence

Retain the loader result; manifest and IMP-0012 comparison record; the
foundation ownership/dependency/gate matrix for R0002, R0004, R0006, and R0008;
the save/preference/cache and local/connected-boundary review; all retained
GATE-0003 / DEC-0003 and GATE-0006 / DEC-0006 records; and the successful
pre-checkpoint validation record. These are contract-review artefacts, not
evidence of implementation, approval, or release readiness.

## Acceptance criteria

- The developer-documentation closure maps every listed foundation prerequisite
  to its retained ownership, dependency boundary, and unresolved gate without
  transferring implementation ownership or claiming acceptance.
- Architecture, command flow, save/recovery, preference/cache separation, and
  P0 connected-scope exclusion remain explicit for both responsive web and PWA
  delivery paths.
- No product asset, implementation, approval, vendor/policy choice, or release
  readiness claim is introduced; GATE-0003 / DEC-0003 and GATE-0006 / DEC-0006
  remain visible.
